WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.202
Now let us run an INNER JOIN.

00:00:02.202 --> 00:00:08.362
In this query, patients receives the alias p and admissions receives the alias a.

00:00:08.362 --> 00:00:14.982
The ON condition says that a row from admissions matches a row from patients when the patient IDs are equal.

00:00:14.982 --> 00:00:18.142
Before running the query, make a prediction.

00:00:18.142 --> 00:00:23.312
Which patients will appear? Patients with at least one admission should appear.

00:00:23.312 --> 00:00:26.317
A patient with no admission should disappear.

00:00:26.317 --> 00:00:29.752
Also think about patients with multiple admissions.

00:00:29.752 --> 00:00:34.812
If one patient has two admissions, that patient should appear on two result rows because each row represents

00:00:34.812 --> 00:00:42.497
one matching admission. Run the query and inspect the patient IDs and admission IDs.

00:00:42.497 --> 00:00:45.747
Try not to focus only on whether the query runs.

00:00:45.747 --> 00:00:48.552
Ask whether the number of rows makes sense.

00:00:48.552 --> 00:00:54.432
If admissions has eight valid admission records, and every admission belongs to a valid patient,

00:00:54.432 --> 00:00:57.647
the joined result should contain about eight rows.

00:00:57.647 --> 00:01:00.852
This same pattern appears in many industries.

00:01:00.852 --> 00:01:06.752
A retailer may join customers with orders to analyze only customers who purchased something.

00:01:06.752 --> 00:01:13.162
A learning platform may join students with completed assessments to analyze only students with activity.

00:01:13.162 --> 00:01:16.604
INNER JOIN is useful when a match is required by the question.
